Centering a `<div>` (or any block‑level element) can be done in a few different ways depending on what you need:

| Goal | CSS technique | Example |
|------|----------------|---------|
| **Horizontal only** (the div keeps its natural height) | `margin: 0 auto;` (needs a set width) | ```html\n<div class="center-horiz">I’m centered horizontally</div>\n```<br>```css\n.center-horiz {\n  width: 300px;          /* any width you want */\n  margin: 0 auto;        /* top/bottom 0, left/right auto */\n}\n``` |
| **Both horizontal & vertical** (full‑page or container) | Flexbox (`display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center;`) | ```html\n<div class="wrapper">\n  <div class="box">Centered both ways</div>\n</div>\n```<br>```css\n.wrapper {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;   /* horizontal */\n  align-items: center;       /* vertical */\n  height: 100vh;             /* make the wrapper fill the viewport */\n}\n.box {\n  width: 200px;\n  padding: 20px;\n  background: #f0f0f0;\n}\n``` |
| **Both horizontal & vertical** (using CSS Grid) | `display: grid; place-items: center;` | ```html\n<div class="grid-wrapper">\n  <div class="box">Centered with Grid</div>\n</div>\n```<br>```css\n.grid-wrapper {\n  display: grid;\n  place-items: center;   /* shorthand for justify-items & align-items */\n  height: 100vh;\n}\n.box { background:#e0e0e0; padding:20px; }\n``` |
| **Both horizontal & vertical** (absolute positioning) | `position: absolute; top: 50%; left: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%);` | ```html\n<div class="relative-parent">\n  <div class="centered-abs">Centered via absolute</div>\n</div>\n```<br>```css\n.relative-parent {\n  position: relative;   /* establishes containing block */\n  height: 400px;        /* any height */\n  background:#fafafa;\n}\n.centered-abs {\n  position: absolute;\n  top: 50%; left: 50%;\n  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);\n  background:#ddd; padding:20px;\n}\n``` |
| **Inline‑block content** (text‑like centering) | `text-align: center;` on the parent | ```html\n<div class="parent">\n  <div class="inline">I’m centered like text</div>\n</div>\n```<br>```css\n.parent { text-align: center; }\n.inline { display: inline-block; }\n``` |

---

## Quick “cheat sheet”

```css
/* 1️⃣ Horizontal only */
.center-horiz {
  width: 300px;          /* required */
  margin: 0 auto;
}

/* 2️⃣ Full‑page flex centering */
.full-flex {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;   /* horizontal */
  align-items: center;       /* vertical */
  height: 100vh;             /* or any container height */
}

/* 3️⃣ Full‑page grid centering */
.full-grid {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;       /* shorthand for both axes */
  height: 100vh;
}

/* 4️⃣ Absolute‑position centering */
.abs-center {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%; left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}

/* 5️⃣ Text‑align centering (for inline/inline‑block) */
.text-center-parent { text-align: center; }
```

---

### Which method should you pick?

| Situation | Recommended method |
|-----------|--------------------|
| You only need **horizontal centering** and the element has a known width | `margin: 0 auto;` |
| You want **both axes** and the container can be a flex container (most modern browsers) | Flexbox (`display:flex`) |
| You already use **CSS Grid** for layout, or you like its brevity | Grid (`place-items:center`) |
| You need to center something **relative to a specific ancestor** that isn’t the whole page, and you don’t want to change that ancestor’s display type | Absolute positioning with `transform` |
| You’re centering **inline‑block or inline elements** (e.g., a button inside a paragraph) | `text-align:center` on the parent |

All of these work in modern browsers (Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari). If you need to support very old browsers (IE 10‑), Flexbox and Grid are still safe, but the absolute‑position trick works everywhere.

---

### A complete minimal example (flex)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Centered Div Demo</title>
<style>
  body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
  .centered {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    height: 100%;               /* fill the viewport */
    background: #f5f5f5;
  }
  .box {
    width: 250px;
    padding: 30px;
    background: #4a90e2;
    color: white;
    text-align: center;
    border-radius: 8px;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="centered">
  <div class="box">I’m perfectly centered!</div>
</div>
</body>
</html>
```

Copy‑paste that into a file, open it in a browser, and you’ll see the blue box centered both horizontally and vertically.

